(a) The city manager or his/her designee shall provide written notice to an alcoholic beverage license applicant of the city manager or his/her designee's decision to deny said alcoholic beverage license application. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify the applicant of the right to appeal under the provisions of this article.
(b) An applicant may appeal the denial of an alcoholic beverage license application by providing written notification to the city manager within ten (10) calendar days of the denial of the alcoholic beverage license application. The written notification shall include the legal and factual basis upon which the applicant's appeal is based.
(c) In order to defray administrative costs, the appeal must be accompanied by a filing fee of five hundred dollars ($500.00). If, following the administrative hearing, the city manager or his/her designee issues an alcoholic beverage license to the applicant, then the five hundred dollar ($500.00) filing fee shall be applied towards the cost of the alcoholic beverage license.
(d) The city manager or his/her designee shall set an administrative hearing date that shall occur within thirty (30) calendar days of the filing of the appeal request. Notice of said administrative hearing shall be mailed to the alcoholic beverage license applicant at the address shown on the alcoholic beverage license applicant's application.
(e) At the administrative hearing, the alcoholic beverage license applicant is entitled to be heard and present evidence on his/her behalf. The city manager or his/her designee shall determine whether the denial of the alcoholic beverage license application is justified using a preponderance of the evidence standard.
(f) The city manager or his/her designee shall consider the following factors in determining whether to uphold the denial of an application:
(1) Compliance with the criminal history requirements;
(2) Compliance with the requirements of this chapter;
(3) The history of and facts relating to previous violations of this chapter;
(4) Non-alcohol related violations of this Code;
(5) Violations of O.C.G.A. Title 3 or of Subtitle 560-2 of the Georgia Regulations pertaining to the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax Division of the Georgia Department of Revenue;
(6) Documented efforts at avoiding violations of this chapter, this Code or state law;
(7) Community involvement; and
(8) Any other objective and relevant facts.
(g) The city manager or his/her designee, after hearing evidence, shall have the authority to uphold, modify or overturn the city manager or his/her designee's previous decision. The city manager or his/her designee shall issue a written decision on the appeal within thirty (30) calendar days of the date of the administrative hearing. All decisions by the city manager or his/her designee shall be mailed to the alcoholic beverage license applicant at the address set forth in the license application.
(h) Any applicant may appeal the city manager or his/her designee's decision to the mayor and city council within ten (10) calendar days of the date that the adverse decision is issued by the city manager. Notice of the appeal shall be delivered in writing to the clerk of the city. The written notification shall include the legal and factual basis upon which the applicant's appeal is based.
(i) In order to defray administrative costs, the appeal must be accompanied by an additional filing fee of five hundred dollars ($500.00). If, following the administrative hearing, the mayor and city council issue an alcoholic beverage license to the applicant, then the one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) in total filing fees shall be applied towards the cost of the alcoholic beverage license.
(j) The mayor and city council shall set a hearing date within thirty (30) calendar days of receiving the appeal request. Notice of said hearing shall be mailed to the applicant at the address shown on the alcoholic beverage license applicant's application. At the hearing, the applicant is entitled to be heard and present evidence on his/her behalf. The city manager or his/her representative may similarly be heard and present evidence in support of the denial. The decision of the city manager or his/her designee shall not be set aside unless the mayor and city council finds it to be:
(1) Contrary to law or ordinances;
(2) Unsupported by substantial evidence on the record as a whole; or
(3) Unreasonable.
(k) All decisions of the mayor and city council shall be final. The mayor and city council shall issue a written decision on the appeal within thirty (30) calendar days of the date of the hearing. All decisions by the city council shall be in writing and shall be mailed to the alcoholic beverage license applicant at the address set forth in the applicable license application.
(Ord. No. 2026-14, § 3(Exh. A), 7-7-26)

