(a) The city manager or his/her designee shall provide written notice to a license holder of the city manager or his/her designee's intention to impose one or more of the following administrative penalties:
(1) Suspend their license;
(2) Revoke their license;
(3) Impose an administrative monetary penalty not to exceed five thousand dollars ($5,000.00); or
(4) Convey a warning for any violation of this chapter, of O.C.G.A. Title 3, or of Subtitle 560-2 of the Georgia Regulations pertaining to the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax Division of the Georgia Department of Revenue.
Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the facts supporting such violation, shall set forth the proposed administrative penalty(ies), and shall notify the license holder of their right to appeal under the provisions of this article. Said written notification shall be mailed to the license holder at the address set forth in the applicable application, and a copy of said written notification shall be hand delivered to the premises where said violation(s) are alleged to have occurred.
(b) A license holder may appeal the proposed administrative penalty(ies) by providing written notification to the city manager within ten (10) calendar days of the date that said written notification was hand delivered to the premises. The written notification shall include the legal and factual basis upon which the applicant's appeal is based.
(c) In the event that the license holder does not timely appeal said proposed administrative penalty(ies), then said license holder shall be deemed to agree with said proposed administrative penalty(ies). Further, in the event that said proposed administrative penalty(ies) include the imposition of an administrative monetary penalty, said administrative monetary penalty shall be due and owing to the city within five (5) calendar days of the date of hand delivery. The failure to timely pay any administrative monetary penalties shall subject the license holder to further administrative penalties.
(d) In the event that the license holder does timely appeal said proposed administrative penalty(ies), then the city manager or his/her designee shall set an administrative hearing date that shall occur within thirty (30) calendar days of the filing of the appeal request. Notice of said administrative hearing shall be mailed to the license holder at the address shown on the license holder's application and a copy of said written notice shall be hand delivered to the premises where said violation(s) are alleged to have occurred.
(e) At the administrative hearing, the license holder is entitled to be heard and present evidence on his/her behalf. The city manager or his/her designee shall determine whether the proposed administrative penalty(ies) of the license holder as set forth in the written notification is justified using a preponderance of the evidence standard.
(f) The city manager or his/her designee shall consider the following factors in determining whether the proposed administrative penalty(ies) of the license holder as set forth in the written notification is justified:
(1) Compliance with the criminal history requirements;
(2) The history of and facts relating to previous violations of this chapter;
(3) Non-alcohol related violations of this Code;
(4) Violations of O.C.G.A. Title 3 or of Subtitle 560-2 of the Georgia Regulations pertaining to the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax Division of the Georgia Department of Revenue;
(5) Documented efforts at avoiding violations of this chapter, of O.C.G.A. Title 3, or of Subtitle 560-2 of the Georgia Regulations pertaining to the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax Division of the Georgia Department of Revenue;
(6) Training history of employees;
(7) Community involvement; and
(8) Any other objective and relevant facts.
(g) The city manager or his/her designee, after hearing evidence, shall have the authority to uphold, modify or overturn the city manager or his/her designee's proposed administrative penalty(ies). The city manager or his/her designee shall issue a written decision on the appeal within thirty (30) calendar days of the date of the hearing. All decisions by the city manager or his/her designee shall be mailed to the license holder at the address set forth in the license holder's application and a copy of said written decision shall be hand delivered to the premises where said violation(s) are alleged to have occurred.
(h) Any license holder may appeal the city manager or his/her designee's decision to the mayor and city council within ten (10) calendar days of the date that the adverse decision is hand delivered to the premises. Notice of the appeal shall be delivered in writing to the clerk of the city. The written notification shall include the legal and factual basis upon which the applicant's appeal is based.
(i) In order to defray administrative costs, the appeal shall be accompanied by a filing fee of five hundred dollars ($500.00). If, following the administrative hearing, the city manager or his/her designee's decision is modified or overturned, then the five hundred dollar ($500.00) filing fee shall be refunded. The mayor and city council shall set a hearing date within thirty (30) calendar days of receiving the appeal request. Notice of said hearing shall be mailed to the applicant at the address shown on the alcoholic beverage license applicant's application and a copy of said notice shall be hand delivered to the premises where said violation(s) are alleged to have occurred. At the hearing, the license holder is entitled to be heard and present evidence on his/her behalf. The city manager or his/her representative may similarly be heard and present evidence in support of the imposition of the administrative penalty(ies). The decision of the city manager or his/her designee shall not be set aside unless the mayor and city council finds it to be:
(1) Contrary to law or ordinances;
(2) Unsupported by substantial evidence on the record as a whole; or
(3) Unreasonable.
(j) All decisions of the mayor and city council shall be final. The mayor and city council shall issue a written decision on the appeal within thirty (30) calendar days of the date of delivery of the hearing. All decisions by the city council shall be in writing and shall be mailed to the license holder at the address set forth in the license holder's application and a copy of said written decision shall be hand delivered to the premises where said violation(s) are alleged to have occurred.
(Ord. No. 2026-14, § 3(Exh. A), 7-7-26)

